WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:06.629
ADHD stands for attention deficit
hyperactivity disorder and is often

00:00:06.629 --> 00:00:12.059
described as a mental disorder. children
with ADHD have trouble paying attention

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:17.490
are hyperactive and often have
difficulty controlling their behavior. it

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:22.260
is estimated that it affects around five
percent of all children aged 3 to 17

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:28.920
globally and that for every girl around
three boys are diagnosed with it. to

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.480
understand how it affects children in
school let's look at the story of Leo, a

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.860
12-year-old boy who goes to school with
the best intentions but struggles hard

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.520
to succeed.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:47.640
attention deficit is his biggest problem.
Leo gets distracted so easily it happens

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.320
even when he tries his hardest to focus.
he often realizes that he has suddenly

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.219
zoned out and has spent the last 15
minutes thinking about something

00:00:56.219 --> 00:01:02.160
entirely different. just the tiniest
thing can get him off track. to him it

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.860
feels like his brain is broken.
he is also forgetful books and homework

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.600
are often left at home and if he doesn't
miss an assignment he often loses it

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.600
somewhere or forgets to turn it in. his
grades are terrible and some teachers

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.900
are beginning to think that he is a lost
case.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.369
then there is his hyperactivity: when
they have to do group work he is

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:33.409
restless and has trouble staying focused.
staying calm and listening while others

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:38.330
speak can completely drain him, making
any normal conversation a serious

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:44.090
challenge. to him it feels like there is
no capacity left in his brain to deal

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.549
with all the input that needs to be
processed.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:52.399
he then feels angry about not being able
to follow along. to help cope with his

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:57.700
hyperactivity he likes to keep his hands
busy all the time.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:03.890
last, there is his impulsive behavior: he
often cannot refrain from saying things

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:09.470
that come to his mind. sometimes he tries
hard to control himself but then just

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:15.620
blurts out and interrupts others. his
classmates find this annoying. he leader

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.540
regrets his hot-headed behavior but he
knows that unfortunately, he will do it

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:26.019
again and again. it seems to him that he
can't learn from his mistakes.

00:02:26.019 --> 00:02:30.859
teachers get frustrated trying to get
him to behave. others become impatient,

00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:38.750
give up or distance themselves. after he
is diagnosed and receives support

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:44.660
through concrete steps, things begin to
get better: at school, he is seated next

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.040
to a supportive student in the front row
of the class, he gets a notebook that

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.440
lists all his assignments to help him
remember and to make homework easier to

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.260
track he receives it for all subjects
only once a week. to relax he is allowed

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.600
to use fidget objects during lessons and
take short breaks when needed. after

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.430
school he practices speaking and
listening routines with the specialist.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:14.060
additionally, his dad bikes with him to
school every morning and in the

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:20.660
afternoon he is allowed to play the ball
as long as he wishes. for severe cases of

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:25.519
ADHD prescription drugs are often
prescribed. before that happens

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:30.019
children like Leo need to undergo a
professional age-appropriate diagnosis

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:34.370
by a child psychologist who will try to
look below the surface.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:39.800
ADHD could just be the tip of the
iceberg the root cause might be drama at

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:47.629
home, bullying at school, poor sleep or
the wrong diet. Sir Ken Robinson told the

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:51.950
story of Gillian Lynne, an 8-year-old
girl that was said to have a learning

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:57.410
disorder. she could not concentrate and
never sat still. when she was brought to

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:01.730
the specialist who didn't subscribe any
therapy but instead played music on the

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:08.569
radio, the girl started dancing. he then
told her mother: "Gillian isn't sick, she's

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:14.209
a dancer. take her to a dance school!"
Gillian Lynne later became a famous

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:18.950
dancer and then responsible for some of
the most successful musicals in Broadway

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:25.520
history. please share your thoughts in
the comments below. if you are

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.100
hyperactive or if you are diagnosed with
ADHD please tell us about your coping

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:36.139
mechanisms so we can learn more about it
from reading your insights. if you want

00:04:36.139 --> 00:04:40.220
to support our Channel, enabling us
to make more such videos, visit

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:46.120
patreon.com/sprouts and check out
what we do

